\title{Security considerations}
\description{
gptr runs a language model agent inside your R session. The agent reads files, writes files,
evaluates R code written by the model in the environment you give it and starts processes on
your behalf. This page describes what gptr does to keep those actions under your control and
where its protections end. \link{gptr_egress} describes what is sent to model providers.
}
\section{Everything happens because you asked}{

When the package is loaded, it only stops the child processes that an earlier gptr session
left running after it was killed or crashed, and removes their records in
\code{tools::R_user_dir("gptr", "cache")}. A model is contacted only when you call \code{\link[=peter]{peter()}} (or a
function that you give a prompt), and model-written code is evaluated only in the
environment you pass as \code{envir} (by default the frame that called \code{\link[=peter]{peter()}}); gptr never
assigns into the global environment by itself. Files are written only into paths you pass to
a gptr function (for example \code{to} in \code{\link[=gptr_export]{gptr_export()}} or \code{paths} in \code{\link[=gptr_scrub]{gptr_scrub()}} with
\code{dry_run = FALSE}), inside the project's \verb{.gptr/} directory once it exists (see
\code{\link[=gptr_init]{gptr_init()}}), inside \code{tools::R_user_dir("gptr")}, inside the session temporary directory,
into files you bound with \code{\link[=gptr_doc]{gptr_doc()}} or confirmed, and by tool calls that the permission
mode allowed.
}

\section{Permission modes}{

Every tool call passes a permission gate. The default mode is \code{manual}: actions that the
static classifier labels read-only run without asking; every other action it recognizes,
including a change to a file or an object, asks first with a one-line prompt. The classifier
is advisory, not a security boundary (see below). \code{edits} also allows file edits inside the
project and still asks before R code that is not known to be read-only; \code{auto}
asks only for level-4 actions (for example deleting the project directory or a file outside the
project, where a system temporary directory such as \verb{/tmp} counts as outside, moving R's working
directory out of the project, or changing gptr's own configuration) and for reads of registered
secrets; \code{yolo}, which only you can select (a project setting or model code in another mode
cannot), also runs gptr's own configuration functions, writes to R startup files such as
\verb{~/.Rprofile} (which can change gptr's options in the next R process) and secret reads without
asking, and still asks before deleting protected files, files outside the project or the
project itself, moving the working directory out of the project, \code{rm(list = ls())},
\verb{options(gptr.*)} and sending a secret over the network; \code{plan} changes nothing. In every mode
\code{r} code that calls \code{q()}, \code{readline()} or another function that would end, pause or hang your R
session is refused without a question. In \code{auto} and \code{yolo} mode a \code{system()} or \code{system2()}
call (on Windows also \code{shell()} or \code{shell.exec()}) whose command the code computes is refused
(see below). When nobody can answer a question (Rscript, knitr, a scheduled job) the run stops
with status \code{blocked} and a \code{gptr_error_permission} condition that says how to allow the action,
unless you set \code{options(gptr.noninteractive_ask = "deny")}. Model code cannot switch the gate
off or loosen it during a run: the safety options are copied when the run starts. Only the option
\code{gptr.unsafe_no_permissions}, set by you outside a run, removes the gate, and it is meant for
disposable sandboxes.

Changing gptr's own configuration includes model code that sets or unsets an environment
variable gptr reads as configuration: a key or base-URL variable of a provider gptr has
registered (such as \code{AWS_REGION}, which places Bedrock's endpoint), or one that moves its
configuration folder or your home directory, such as \code{HOME}; \link{gptr_options} lists them. The
classifier reads such a change in \code{Sys.setenv()}, \code{Sys.unsetenv()}, \code{withr::local_envvar()} and
\code{withr::with_envvar()}, where a name it cannot read counts as one (also one built with \code{paste0()}
or another helper of base R that the code or your session replaces), in \code{readRenviron()} and
\code{dotenv::load_dot_env()}, and in an \code{os.environ} write in the code of \code{peter$py()} or
\code{peter$knit("python", code)} that names one in a string or an upper-case word, or whose name (a
subscript, or the first argument of \code{putenv()}, \code{unsetenv()} or \code{pop()}) is not a plain string
literal. It does not read code in a file R runs (\code{source()}, \code{sys.source()},
\code{eval(parse(file = ))}); \code{Sys.setenv()}, \code{Sys.unsetenv()}, \code{withr::local_envvar()} or
\code{withr::with_envvar()} reached by a computed name, through a list or an environment, or handed to
another function (\code{get("Sys.unsetenv")}, \code{baseenv()$Sys.setenv},
\code{asNamespace("withr")$local_envvar}, \code{with(list(f = withr::with_envvar), ...)},
\code{assign("f", Sys.unsetenv)}, \code{identity(withr::local_envvar)}); a Python write that reaches
\code{os.environ} by another name (\code{e = os.environ}, \code{getattr(os, "environ")}) or through \code{ctypes}, or
that passes \code{os.environ.update()} a mapping the code computes or keyword names not all in upper
case (on Windows \code{os.environ.update(home = d)} sets \code{HOME}); or Python run through 'reticulate':
such code is level 3 or lower, which \code{auto} runs without asking.
}

\section{What the gate cannot do}{

The risk levels come from a static classifier (\code{\link[=gptr_risk]{gptr_risk()}}). It is advisory and it is not a
security boundary: R code can compute function names, call compiled code or load packages
whose effects the classifier cannot see, and R has no sandbox for code that runs inside your
session. The classifier reads code as written and does not run it, so code that builds a call,
a name, a path or a command at run time, or reaches a function by another name, is read only
as far as the classifier can see. Each form this page names as not read is of that kind, or is
code in a file or a package, code another interpreter runs, or a call or argument the
classifier has no rule for. Deny and ask \verb{sh:} rules see a command computed at run time only in
a \code{peter$sh()} or \code{peter$bg()} call, which checks the command when it runs. So in \code{auto} and
\code{yolo} mode a \code{system()}, \code{system2()}, \code{shell()} or \code{shell.exec()} call whose command (for
\code{system2()} also its arguments and \code{env}) is not a string the classifier can read, such as
\verb{cmd = paste("rm", "-rf", "build"); system(cmd)}, is refused, and the model is told to run the
command with \code{peter$sh()}; \code{manual} and \code{edits} ask. An allow rule such as \code{r(fn:system)} lets
such calls run, unchecked by \verb{sh:} rules. Neither the rules nor the refusal read what a shell
reads when it runs: its standard input (\code{system("sh", input = cmd)},
\code{peter$sh("sh", input = cmd)}), variables it expands (\verb{sh -c "$X"}, \code{eval $X}) or a script file.
The refusal covers only calls the classifier resolves to these functions: a function reached by
a computed name or through a list or an environment (\code{get("system")(cmd)},
\code{baseenv()$system(cmd)}) or handed to a function of your own (\code{g(system)}), \code{eval(parse())}, a
sourced file, package code and other command runners such as \code{pipe()} or \code{processx::run()}
still run a computed command unchecked. A literal command is read when it is given to
\code{system()}, \code{system2()}, \code{peter$sh()}, \code{processx::run()} (also as \code{callr::run()}),
\code{sys::exec_wait()}, \code{sys::exec_internal()} or \code{sys::exec_background()}, not when it is given to
\code{processx::process$new()}, which is level 3.

Paths are placed in the project: a relative path in R code is read in the project directory, and
a command in the directory it runs in (after a \code{cd} or \code{env -C}, or the \code{wd} of \code{peter$sh()} or
\code{processx::run()}), the code a shell or Python runs there (\code{sh -c}, \code{cmd /c}, \code{pwsh -Command},
\code{python3 -c}) included; running a command in a directory outside the project reads that
directory, so \code{manual} asks. \code{setwd()}, \code{withr::local_dir()}, \code{withr::with_dir()} and
\code{xfun::in_dir()} to a directory outside the project and R's session temporary directory, or to
one the code computes, are level 4, because a relative path would then name a file outside the
project, and so are \code{file.symlink()}, \code{file.link()}, \code{Sys.junction()} and \code{fs::link_create()} of
a path outside the project, as \code{ln} of one is. \code{getwd()} and the directory \code{setwd()} returns are
read as the one the code has moved R to, starting from R's working directory when the classifier
reads the code, so at the top level of the code \verb{owd = setwd("data"); ...; setwd(owd)} stays in
the project. A drive-relative path such as \code{D:data} counts as outside on every OS. The classifier
does not follow the working directory changed in other ways (such as \code{usethis::local_project()},
\code{get("setwd")} or the \code{wd} of \code{callr::r()}), nor read the code another interpreter runs
(\code{Rscript -e}, \code{perl -e}, \code{node -e}), a path the shell computes (\verb{rm "$f"} in a loop, \verb{xargs rm})
or code handed to \code{sh -c} or \code{bash -c} as one word whose spaces are escaped with a backslash or
written \verb{$IFS} (\verb{sh -c rm\\ -f\\ file}): such a command is level 3, which \code{auto} and \code{yolo} run
without a question whatever it deletes. The \code{wd} of \code{processx::run()} is read when the call, or a
name the code assigns, holds it as a string; a \code{wd} the code computes is read as the project
directory.

In \code{auto} and \code{yolo} mode a model can delete or overwrite files and objects. Use them only in
projects you can restore (version control, backups, \code{\link[=gptr_rewind]{gptr_rewind()}} for recent turns), and run
untrusted prompts or untrusted projects in a disposable container or virtual machine.
}

\section{Sub-agents, workers and child processes}{

Inline sub-agents run in your session and see its objects. The worker backend runs a
sub-agent in a separate R process that starts from gptr's reduced child environment (it does
not read your \verb{~/.Renviron}), but that process runs under your user account with your file
permissions: the worker backend is not an isolation boundary. Its permission requests are
sent to your session and classified again there. On the \code{codex} route Codex's own shell runs
commands in every mode without passing gptr's gate, rules or secret guard: in \code{plan}, \code{manual}
and \code{edits} its sandbox is read-only, so it can still read any file your account can read, and
in \code{auto} and \code{yolo} it also changes and deletes files inside the project within its sandbox.
MCP servers, the \code{claude} and \code{codex} command-line tools, shell
commands started with \code{peter$sh()} and Shiny artifacts are ordinary processes of your account
as well; artifacts start without any registered secret and listen
on the loopback interface, and each launch gets an access token in its address (on Windows
only when 'openssl' is installed; otherwise gptr says that the artifact has no token).
}

\section{Untrusted content}{

Model replies, tool results, file contents, MCP results and project instruction files
(\code{AGENTS.md}, \code{CLAUDE.md}, \code{.gptr/vignette.Rmd}) are treated as data. Text from these sources
is never used as a formatting template, so braces in a reply cannot run R code. A project you
cloned is untrusted until you trust it with \code{gptr_trust(trust = TRUE)} (see \code{\link[=gptr_trust]{gptr_trust()}}),
or answer yes when \code{\link[=gptr_init]{gptr_init()}} asks: until then its settings can only tighten
permissions, its extensions, plugins and MCP servers do not run, and its instruction files
are shown to the model as information rather than commands. When trusted files change after a
pull, gptr asks again.
}

\section{Secrets}{

Keys are held in a private vault and appear in sessions, logs and errors only as markers such
as \verb{[secret:ANTHROPIC_API_KEY]}. Values are redacted before anything is written or sent: the
session files, documents, caches, spill files, wire logs and the output of child processes.
gptr never reads the credential files of other tools. Reading a registered secret from model
code asks even in \code{auto} mode. So does handing one to a child process through the \code{env} of
\code{peter$sh()} or \code{peter$script()}, by its name or in a value, unless the same code reads that
secret by name: the question for that read, or an \code{r(secret:NAME)} rule allowing it, covers
the hand-over too. A key registered after it already appeared in a file (for example pasted
into a prompt) can be removed from the files with \code{\link[=gptr_scrub]{gptr_scrub()}}; run
\code{gptr_scrub(error = TRUE)} before committing to fail when any registered secret remains.
}

\section{Data at rest and protected health information (PHI)}{

gptr keeps what it needs to resume and replay work. Review what you commit when the data are
personal or clinical:
\itemize{
\item \verb{.gptr/sessions/} (full conversations, including printed results) is excluded from git by
the \code{.gptr/.gitignore} that \code{\link[=gptr_init]{gptr_init()}} writes.
\item \verb{.gptr/cache/s1/} holds System 1 answers and is committed by default so that a script
replays without model calls. It stores a salted hash of each input and a hash of each
question, never their text, but the answers themselves (a label and its probability)
remain, and the salt is committed with the cache, so anyone with the repository can test
guesses of short inputs. For PHI, stop committing it with
\code{gptr_config(cache_commit = list(s1 = FALSE, s2 = FALSE), .scope = "project")} (gptr then
keeps a \code{.gitignore} in \verb{cache/s1/}), or clear it with \code{gptr_cache("clear", "s1")} before
committing.
\item \verb{.gptr/cache/s2/} (answer text for replay), \verb{.gptr/transcripts/} (console transcripts),
\verb{.gptr/checkpoints/} (pre-images of objects and files) and the data snapshots of artifacts
(\verb{.gptr/artifacts/<id>/v<n>/data/}) are excluded from git by default.
\item \verb{.gptr/plans/} (plans proposed in \code{plan} mode) and the \code{app.R} of each artifact are
committed by default; they can quote data values the model saw.
\item Documents you record into keep the model's code and up to \code{gptr.doc_output_lines} lines of
printed output per step, which can contain data values. A console turn's block also keeps,
as \verb{#< } lines, what its prompt was sent with, whatever that option says: the head of each
\verb{@file} (up to 40 lines) and the \code{!} code notes with their printed output. Review them before
sharing.
}
}

\section{Local servers}{

\code{\link[=gptr_mcp_serve]{gptr_mcp_serve()}} exposes the live session to other agents over HTTP on \verb{127.0.0.1} only,
with a random bearer token and an \code{Origin} check, and every call passes the permission gate.
Any local process that learns the token can call the served tools while the server runs; stop
it with \code{gptr_mcp_serve(stop = TRUE)} when you are done.
}

